## Live-sync the availability calendar

### Status
Not started.

### GitHub Issue
#91

### Summary
Add real-time sync (via ASP.NET Core SignalR) so every connected viewer of an event's Home page
sees availability toggles, celebrations, and final-date changes live, without a manual refresh —
and so the Finalize page's Final Dates card stays live-synced too. Today the calendar (grid cells,
legend, Best Bets card, Status card, Final Dates card) only ever updates from the current browser's
own fetch responses; nothing pushes a change to any other participant viewing the same event.

### Motivation
Two visible symptoms today:
- When a date's toggled to "everyone's in," the confetti/pop celebration only plays for whoever
  just clicked — anyone else viewing the page never sees it.
- When an organizer chooses a final date (from the Finalize page), it doesn't appear on anyone
  else's Home page — including the organizer's own, on a different tab — until they manually
  refresh. And when it does appear, it should be exactly as celebratory as a date completing —
  an organizer choosing the date is as much a "we're locking this in" moment as the group finishing
  it themselves — but today nothing plays for it at all.

---

### Design Decisions
These were worked out collaboratively before any code was written (see conversation on this spec's
creation date):

- **Transport:** SignalR, ships with ASP.NET Core, no new package. One hub, one group per event,
  keyed by the event's **code** (matches the URL/route already; no extra id lookup needed
  client-side).
- **Trust model:** Same as the rest of the app today — no new auth layer. Anyone who can load the
  event Home/Finalize page (i.e. anyone with the code) can join its SignalR group, consistent with
  how joining/viewing already works. Participants are not ASP.NET Identity-authenticated users.
- **Live delivery — broadcasts carry the actual data, not just a "go refetch" signal.** The
  existing client (`Views/Events/Home.cshtml`) already renders entirely off a local JS `calendar`
  object mutated in place (`datesByKey`, `renderBestBets()`, `renderStatus()`, `renderFinalDates()`,
  `celebrateDate()`); `ToggleAvailability` already returns the exact `{date, participantIds}` shape
  needed. `AddFinalDate`/`RemoveFinalDate` currently `RedirectToRoute` with no JSON response at
  all, so their broadcast necessarily carries the updated final-dates data too. This reuses
  existing payload shapes almost as-is instead of adding a new "signal only" endpoint that every
  viewer would have to round-trip against.
- **Self-echo:** The server excludes the acting connection from its own broadcast
  (`Clients.GroupExcept(callerConnectionId)`). The acting tab already applied the update from its
  own direct response; that same participant's *other* open tabs still receive it live via the
  broadcast.
- **No attribution.** Broadcasts/UI never say who made a change — silent state updates only, per
  the issue's original scope.
- **Reconnect/catch-up:** SignalR's built-in `withAutomaticReconnect`, plus one snapshot refetch on
  reconnect via a small JSON endpoint. Scoped to calendar/final-dates state for this pass, but
  intentionally shaped as a reusable pattern (a snapshot endpoint for catch-up, separate from the
  live-broadcast path) for the chat system's eventual live-sync, not built now.
- **Per-page/per-element scope:**
  - Home page: every calendar-driven piece live-updates — grid cells (wedges, pink/blue borders),
    legend, Best Bets card, Status card, Final Dates card, and the confetti/pop celebration, for
    every connected viewer. The celebration plays for both triggers: a date's availability
    reaching "everyone's in", and a date newly becoming final — each viewer's own reconnect
    catch-up snapshot (see below) never replays it, same as it never does for availability.
  - Finalize page: **only** `_FinalizeFinalDatesCard.cshtml` live-updates.
    `_FinalizeCallTheDateCard.cshtml` (the date-range picker/form) is never touched by a broadcast
    — an organizer actively filling out that form shouldn't have it change under them.
  - Both Home and Finalize open a hub connection and join the event's group.
- **Final-date conflict detection (new, folded into this issue):** `AddFinalDate`/`RemoveFinalDate`
  gain a concurrency check. An organizer's submit is rejected with a clear error (Finalize
  redisplayed with a ModelState error, e.g. "Final dates changed — review and try again") if the
  final-dates list changed server-side since their page loaded, rather than silently overwriting.
  Scoped narrowly: the client submits the set of final-date ids it was showing at load time (a
  hidden field), and the server rejects if the DB's current id set differs at save time — **no new
  column, no migration.** This is deliberately not the general "track event last-active" concept
  the developer is separately planning as its own future issue; that stays out of scope here, and
  this narrow mechanism is not assumed to be its foundation.
- **Rollout:** One pass — everything above ships together, not staged.

---

### Proposed Changes

#### Hub
- New `EventHub : Hub` (suggested location: `Hubs/EventHub.cs`, new folder — small, single
  purpose, matches `Services/`' precedent of focused files).
- `JoinEvent(string code)` / connection group management: adds the caller's connection to a group
  named after the event code on connect; SignalR handles per-connection cleanup on disconnect.
- No custom authorization — same trust model as the rest of the app (see Design Decisions).

#### Server-side broadcasts
- `EventsController.Availability.cs`'s `ToggleAvailability`: after `SaveChangesAsync`, broadcasts
  `{date, participantIds}` to the event's group, excluding the caller's own connection.
- `EventsController.FinalDate.cs`'s `AddFinalDate`/`RemoveFinalDate`: after `SaveChangesAsync`,
  broadcast the updated final-dates list to the event's group (no exclusion needed here in the same
  way, since the actor's own page reloads via the existing redirect regardless).
- Both actions gain the id-set concurrency check described above before applying the write.

#### Snapshot endpoint (reconnect catch-up)
- New lightweight JSON endpoint returning current calendar/final-dates state, called once by the
  client on reconnect (not used for the live-update path itself).

#### Client (`Home.cshtml`, `Finalize.cshtml` / `_FinalizeFinalDatesCard.cshtml`)
- Establish a SignalR connection on page load, join the event's group, `withAutomaticReconnect`.
- On reconnect, call the snapshot endpoint once and reconcile local state.
- Home: wire the hub's broadcast handlers into the existing `datesByKey` mutation +
  `applyWedges()`/`updateCellCompletion()`/`celebrateDate()`/`renderBestBets()`/`renderStatus()`/
  `renderFinalDates()` functions — the same code path `toggleDate()`'s own fetch response already
  uses. The `FinalDatesChanged` handler also calls `celebrateDate()` for any date newly present in
  the broadcast's final-dates list (diffed against the previous list), so finalizing a date
  celebrates on every connected Home viewer the same way completing one does.
- Finalize: wire only a handler that re-renders `_FinalizeFinalDatesCard`'s content from the
  broadcast payload; the Call-the-Date card's form/state is left untouched by any broadcast.
- `AddFinalDate`/`RemoveFinalDate` forms gain a hidden field carrying the final-date id set the
  page was showing at load, for the concurrency check.

---

### Acceptance Criteria
- Two browsers viewing the same event's Home page: toggling a date in one shows the updated
  wedge/border, legend, Best Bets, and Status card in the other without a manual refresh.
- When a date becomes "everyone's in" from one viewer's toggle, the confetti/pop celebration plays
  for every connected viewer, not just the one who clicked.
- An organizer adding or removing a final date (from Finalize) live-updates the Final Dates card on
  every connected viewer's Home page and every connected Finalize page — including the organizer's
  own other open tab — without a refresh. The Finalize page's Call-the-Date card never changes as a
  result of a broadcast.
- When an organizer adds a final date, the confetti/pop celebration plays on that date's cell for
  every connected Home viewer, the same as an "everyone's in" availability completion. Removing a
  final date plays no celebration. A viewer's reconnect catch-up never replays a celebration for a
  final date that became final while they were disconnected.
- A participant's own acting tab does not receive/re-apply its own change as a redundant broadcast;
  that participant's other open tabs do.
- A dropped-then-restored connection auto-reconnects and reconciles via one snapshot fetch, without
  requiring a manual page refresh to catch up on missed changes.
- Submitting an Add/Remove Final Date action against a stale final-dates list (changed server-side
  since page load) is rejected with a clear on-page error rather than silently applied; a submit
  against a current list succeeds normally.
- No broadcast or UI surface reveals which participant made a change.

### Out of Scope
- The general "track event last-active" concept (e.g. an `Event.LastActiveUtc`-style column) —
  stays a separate future issue, not built or assumed here.
- Chat system live-sync (`EventMessage` has no controller actions/views yet at all) — this issue
  only shapes the snapshot-endpoint pattern to be reusable later, it doesn't build chat's version.
- Attribution/toasts naming who made a change.
- A SignalR scale-out backplane (e.g. Redis) — single-server assumption, matches current hosting.
- Tests — per CLAUDE.md, added only on separate explicit request.
